w maybe the fact that i have success  with languages encourages me to work  hard  uh however  i also know people  many people who are unilingual raised  unilingually  and become very good speakers of two  three four or more other languages  so it's it's undoubtedly and i should  add i know uh people  who grew up in a multilingual  environment and who do not become good  language learners  i know friends that i went to school  with who were in a similar situation who  had emigrated from europe at a similar  age and who essentially did not become  good language learners  a lot of for example quebecois grow up  hearing english and french and they have  two languages and my experience in the  foreign service was that they were not  necessarily better at learning chinese  or japanese than english canadians who  had been raised unilingually  i see examples here in vancouver of kids  who come here  immigrants  say from china kids are born here  they end up in esl at school because  they don't speak english well enough  to to be in a class with the other kids  although to my mind a lot of the esl is  quite unnecessary but but they have  trouble with english and they also don't  have an advantage in learning third  languages  so you know it's another one of these  things that in theory it's an advantage  but it's not a condition  it doesn't guarantee success and the  fact that you haven't had that  opportunity doesn't prevent you from  having success  the the ultimately the language learning  success depends on motivation  and so a very motivated person  regardless of their language background  in childhood will be successful in  language learning it's like a number of  other conditions that in theory are  advantages but which are not conditions  for success for example  people say well if you go to the country  then you'll become fluent  i have met  if i think of the people that i know  say in china or japan  or people that i speak to via skype here  at link  who are russian or japanese or or  spanish  and if i compare their english to the  english of many immigrants i would  almost say the average japanese chinese  russian immigrant that i encounter here  in canada  the people that i got to know  in china japan or talking about spoke  english better  than the immigrants here now i'm not  saying that all people in japan china  russia speak better but i'm saying that  those people  in those countries who have never set  foot in an english-speaking country but  who are very motivated to learn  they end up speaking better than the  average immigrant here so the point  again is that while it's an advantage to  be in the country where the language is  spoken it's not a condition  it doesn't guarantee you success and not  being there doesn't prevent success  because ultimately it boils down to  motivation  now the other thing is the other  question is well my approach to language  learning which is based on a lot of  input you know reading and listening and  vocabulary growth is that something that  only works for someone with my you know  background as a child  uh here again i don't think so uh i  always say the main thing in language  learning is to do what you like to do  but uh i think  no matter what our background is we do  have to get the language in us and  the question of when we start to speak  and how much emphasis we should put on  grammar will depend on the individual  it'll also depend on the opportunity and  you know with russian i i'm not gonna  change my social and professional life  around so that i spend the day with  russian speakers  as susannah suggests that i find a  russian to go grocery shopping with i'm  not about to do that  so therefore i'm stuck with a lot of  listing and reading if i were in russia  yeah i'd be speaking every day with  anyone that i could inflict myself upon  so  yeah i in summary then i think the fact  that i had  from a language learner's perspective an  advantageous  uh  you know background 